+## Subtopics
+
+### SH Degree Reduction (General)
+Reducing SH degree from 3 to 2 or 1. Degree 3→2 reduces parameters by 36% and computation by 35% with a 0.17 dB PSNR drop. Degree 2→1 reduces parameters by 25% and computation by 17% with a 0.31 dB PSNR drop. Degree 3→1 reduces parameters by 61% and computation by 52% with a 0.48 dB PSNR drop [S1].
+
+### Mobile-GS
+A specific implementation of first-order SH distillation targeting mobile devices, combining distillation with neural vector quantization and pruning for extreme compression [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Memory:** Reduces SH storage from 16 coefficients per channel to 4 coefficients per channel, a 75% reduction in SH parameters per Gaussian [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Computation:** Reduces rendering computation by 52% when distilling directly from degree 3 to 1 [S1].
+- Reducing SH degree from 3 to 1 reduces the number of SH coefficients per Gaussian from 48 to 12 (3 channels $\times$ 4 coefficients), resulting in a 61% reduction in parameters and 52% reduction in computation [S1].
+- The distillation process itself adds training overhead due to the need to render from the teacher model [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Measured Performance:**
-*   **Degree 3→2:** -21 elements, -36% parameters, -35% computation, -0.17 dB PSNR [S1].
-*   **Degree 2→1:** -15 elements, -25% parameters, -17% computation, -0.31 dB PSNR [S1].
-*   **Degree 3→1:** -36 elements, -61% parameters, -52% computation, -0.48 dB PSNR [S1].
-*   **Overall:** When combined with other compression methods (like VQ and pruning), SH distillation contributes to a 51.6× overall compression with a 0.73 dB PSNR drop [S1].
+- On standard datasets, first-order SH distillation (3→1) results in a 0.48 dB PSNR drop compared to the original third-order SH [S1].
+- When combined with other compression methods like Vector Quantization (VQ), the overall model can achieve up to 51.6× compression with a 0.73 dB PSNR drop [S1].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduces storage burden and increases rendering speed, making it suitable for mobile devices [S2, unofficial]. Maintains higher fidelity than naive degree reduction due to distillation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Involves a two-stage training process (teacher then student), increasing total training time [G]. There is a trade-off between compression and quality, with a 0.48 dB PSNR drop for 3→1 distillation [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Significantly reduces storage burden and increases rendering speed, making it suitable for mobile and edge devices [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Loss of high-frequency view-dependent details compared to third-order SH, resulting in a measurable PSNR drop [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 **When to Use:**
-*   Use when deploying 3DGS on resource-constrained devices (mobile, edge) where rendering speed and memory footprint are critical [S2, unofficial].
-*   Use when a high-quality teacher model is available for distillation [S2, unofficial].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Use when maximum possible rendering quality is required and computational resources are abundant [G].
-*   Use when a single-stage training pipeline is preferred [G].
